Qus.6 -> What are IDs and classes in HTML, and how do they differ?
Sol -> In HTML, IDs and classes are attributes used to identify and style elements using CSS or JavaScript.

1. ID (id)

   Unique identifier for a single HTML element.
   No two elements should have the same ID.
   Used when you need to target one specific element.
   Can be referenced in CSS using #id-name.

2. Class (class)

   Can be assigned to multiple elements.
   Used when multiple elements share the same styling.
   Can be referenced in CSS using .class-name.

Key Differences Between ID and Class

    Uniqueness – An id is unique and used for a single element, whereas a class can be applied to multiple elements.
    Usage – An id is used for specific element styling or JavaScript targeting, while a class is used for styling or behavior that applies to multiple elements.
    CSS Selector – An id is selected using #id-name, and a class is selected using .class-name.
    JavaScript Selection – The id is accessed using document.getElementById(), while the class is accessed using document.getElementsByClassName().

Both id and class are essential for structuring, styling, and scripting in web development.
